\H{privacy-joliputty} JoliPuTTY

JoliPuTTY is one of those modified versions. What this appendix says
about PuTTY is true of JoliPuTTY too, which changes nothing in the way
PuTTY connects to servers; but JoliPuTTY stores a little more on your
computer, and in a few cases makes a connection of its own, as
described here. Like PuTTY, JoliPuTTY never \q{phones home}: neither
its author nor anyone else collects any information about you or your
sessions.

\S{privacy-jp-local} What JoliPuTTY stores locally

Along with PuTTY's own data, JoliPuTTY stores, in the registry, under
PuTTY's key (in \cw{WinUI}):

\b which saved sessions are your favorites, and when you last used
each of them (for the Recent section of the Sessions page);

\b the folders you've made that are still empty, and which sections
and folders of the Sessions page you've folded away;

\b the application theme.

\S{privacy-jp-pools} Pools of copied text

Text that you keep, with \q{Copy to Pool}, for a saved session or for
all sessions (see \k{jp-pools}) is stored in the registry, encrypted
for your Windows account with Windows' Data Protection API, so that
other users of the computer can't read it. Anything running under your
own account can, though, as it can read anything else you can.

Everything you copy also goes in the tab's history, which is only kept
in memory, and forgotten when the tab is closed. To keep nothing at
all, set \q{Copies to keep} to 0 on the Terminal page of the session's
settings.

\S{privacy-jp-autologon} Automate logon

The commands of Automate logon (\k{jp-autologon}) are saved with the
rest of the session's settings, in the registry, in clear, unless they
are hidden: hidden commands (passwords, mostly) are kept in the
Windows Credential Manager instead, under names starting
\cq{PuTTY Automate logon/}. Think twice before putting a password in
a command, hidden or not: anyone who can use your Windows account can
get it back.

\S{privacy-jp-cleanup} Deleting it all

The \c{\-cleanup} command-line option (\k{using-cleanup}) deletes all
of JoliPuTTY's data in the registry along with PuTTY's, including the
pools of copied text, and the hidden commands of Automate logon from
the Windows Credential Manager.

If you installed JoliPuTTY as an MSIX package, uninstalling it removes
the program, but not your saved sessions, which are where PuTTY keeps
them, so that PuTTY can still use them.

\S{privacy-jp-network} Network connections

JoliPuTTY only connects where PuTTY would, with these additions:

\b If the Windows App Runtime that JoliPuTTY needs isn't installed,
JoliPuTTY offers to open Microsoft's web page to download it. It only
does so if you agree.

\b The \q{Web site} link of the About box opens JoliPuTTY's web page,
in your browser, when you click it.

\b \q{Send File} and \q{Receive File} (\k{jp-termmenu}) transfer files
over the SSH connection that's already open to the server, and
nowhere else.

\b If you installed JoliPuTTY from the Microsoft Store, the Store
keeps it up to date, as it does for its other applications.

When it connects to an SSH server, JoliPuTTY tells it its name and
version (\cw{JoliPuTTY_0.1}) instead of PuTTY's, as every SSH client
tells servers its own.
